<h2>How a Repair Visit Works</h2>
<h3>Diagnosis</h3>
<p>We start at the transformer: input voltage, output on each tap, timer and photocell operation, and whether the unit is appropriately sized for the connected load. Then we measure voltage at the first and last fixture on each run, which tells us immediately whether the problem is a connection, a cable fault or an undersized circuit. A cable fault locator finds breaks and shorts without digging up the whole bed.</p>
<h3>Repair</h3>
<p>Every connection we open is remade with a waterproof gel-filled connector. Cable breaks are spliced with direct-burial rated splices and re-buried at depth. Failed lamps are replaced with 2700K LEDs matched to the rest of the system so the yard does not end up with three colors of white. Fixtures that are beyond saving are replaced with brass units on the existing cable; we will tell you honestly when a lamp swap is enough and when the fixture body has failed.</p>
<h3>Re-aim and reset</h3>
<p>Before we leave, we re-aim fixtures that have been knocked or grown over, clean lenses, reset the timer and confirm every fixture is lit. For an older halogen system, the repair visit is often the right time to discuss an <a href="/services/led-upgrade-and-retrofit/">LED upgrade</a>, since re-lamping twenty halogens one more time rarely makes sense.</p>

